x86_64-linux  The closure type's own name, in all six closures, and nothing else in the case. [expr.prim.lambda] makes a closure a unique unnamed type, so there is no name either compiler is obliged to match: cxx1 spells its display tag `3$_0` where clang writes the Itanium closure encoding `UlvE_` - `_ZZN1S5readsEvENK3$_0clEv` against `_ZZN1S5readsEvENKUlvE_clEv`. Every enclosing function name agrees, the const one included (`ZNK1S11callsMemberEv`), and so does every name the classes themselves have. Nothing links against a closure's call operator: it is reached through the object, and the object never leaves the function that made it.
x86_64-windows  The same one difference, plus the two halves this ABI adds and lambda-capture-this.nocl already records: clang writes the closure `<lambda_1>` at local-scope index `?0` where cxx1 writes `$_0` at `?1`, and clang encodes the call operator's deduced return type as `@` where cxx1 writes the type it deduced - `??R<lambda_1>@?0??reads@S@@QEAAHXZ@QEBA@XZ` against `??R$_0@?1??reads@S@@QEAAHXZ@QEBAHXZ`. The enclosing member function's own name is identical in all six, `?writes@S@@QEAAHXZ` and the rest.
arm64-darwin  The same closure-name difference as x86_64-linux with Mach-O's leading underscore, `__ZZN1S5readsEvENK3$_0clEv` against `__ZZN1S5readsEvENKUlvE_clEv`. Every other name in the case agrees.
